 ],
 "literal": "To us one thing he does not say, even though, to himself one thing / painful night's sweet companion who became / garden Vēṅkai tree, did he forget? / Forgetting difficult bamboo-like shoulders having embraced / abandoning he who is capable of, [is he waiting for] the bird's mouth's messenger?",
 "translation": "Even though he does not say a single word to us, did he forget the Vēṅkai tree in our garden which became his sweet companion during those painful nights? He who is capable of embracing my bamboo-like shoulders, so hard to forget, and then abandoning me, is he now waiting for the bird's voice as a messenger?",
 "modern": "அவர் நமக்கு ஒரு வார்த்தையும் சொல்லவில்லை என்றாலும், துன்பம் தரும் இரவுகளில் தனக்கு இனிய துணையாக இருந்த நம் வீட்டுத் தோட்டத்து வேங்கை மரத்தை மறந்துவிட்டாரோ? மறப்பதற்கு அரிய என் மூங்கில் போன்ற தோள்களைத் தழுவிவிட்டு, பின் என்னைப் பிரிந்து செல்லத் துணிந்த அவர், பறவையின் குரலாகிய தூதை எதிர்பார்த்துக் கொண்டிருக்கிறாரோ?",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to her friend in the pālai (wasteland) landscape, expressing her deep sorrow over the hero's departure. She wonders if the hero, who left without saying a word, has forgotten the Vēṅkai tree in their garden. This tree had been his sweet companion during the painful nights when he secretly waited to meet her. She questions how he could so easily abandon her after embracing her beautiful, bamboo-like shoulders, which should be impossible to forget. The mention of the 'bird's voice as a messenger' suggests he might be waiting for a favorable omen to return, highlighting the irony of his silence.",
